course: dictionary
lesson: 1325
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, so the sentence tells you which one you need. Calico is a good example. Look at the words around it before you decide what it means.
explain: In the UK, calico is a plain, cheap cotton cloth, usually cream or white and not dyed. Designers use it to practise sewing before they cut into good material. If the sentence talks about sewing, bags or practice, calico means the cloth.
explain: In American English, calico can also mean a cotton cloth with small bright flower patterns, or a calico cat, which has patches of white, black and orange fur. Read the clues: if the sentence is about cats, fur or colours, it means the cat. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Look at this sentence: «almost all tortoiseshell and calico cats are female.» Calico sits beside tortoiseshell, and the sentence is about cat colours, so here calico is a cat. The clues tell you the meaning, even when you have never seen the word before.
fact: Calico is a noun. In the UK it is uncountable, so you say some calico, not a calico, when you mean the cloth.
fact: Calico came from the city of Calicut in India, and it was traded around the world in history.
fact: «Calico» means a plain, cheap cotton cloth in the UK, and a patterned cotton cloth with small flowers in the US.
fact: A calico cat has patches of white, black and orange fur. Almost all calico cats are female.
fact: In American pioneer stories, girls often wore calico dresses.
exercise: In design and technology, the class sews a practice bag from calico before using good cloth. What is calico here? | A cat with orange patches | A kind of paint | A city in India | *A plain, cheap cotton cloth
exercise: «Her grandmother's cat is a calico with white, black and orange patches.» What is a calico here? | *A cat with patches of white, black and orange fur | A pioneer girl's dress | A dog with spots | A patterned cotton cloth
exercise: Where did calico get its name? | From an old Latin word for white | From a famous English painter | *From the city of Calicut in India | From a town in America
test: «The pioneer girl sewed a new calico dress for the fair.» Which meaning fits best? | A type of bread | *A patterned cotton cloth with small flowers | A plain, grey stone | A cat with patches of fur
test: Ella says calico always means a cat. Ben says it can mean cloth too. Which is right? | Both, because calico is a town in India | Ella, because calico is only ever a cat | *Ben, because the meaning depends on the clues in the sentence | Neither, because calico is a colour
exam: «Almost all calico cats are female, so the calico in the pet shop was a she.» What does calico mean in this sentence? | *A cat with patches of white, black and orange fur | A plain cotton cloth | A city in India | A type of bread
